const phonologyCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Phoneme: the sound that makes meaning', note: 'In English, [p] and [b] distinguish pat/bat — separate phonemes. In Arabic, [p]/[b] are one phoneme (the same word). Phonemes are defined per language: the minimal meaning-distinguishing sound units.' },
    { math: '2 · Allophones: the costume changes', note: 'The same phoneme varies by position: English [p] is aspirated (puffed) in "pin", unaspirated in "spin" — heard as the same sound by English speakers, as different sounds by Thai speakers. Allophones are the costume; the phoneme is the character.' },
    { math: '3 · Rules are systematic', note: 'English plural: /s/ after voiceless sounds (cats), /z/ after voiced (dogs), /ɪz/ after sibilants (buses). Not memorised per word — a rule applies across the lexicon, including nonsense words (you know "wugs" pluralises with /z/ — the wug test).' },
    { math: '4 · Loanwords expose the system', note: 'Japanese adds a vowel to consonant clusters ("sutoraiku" for strike) because its phonology forbids clusters. Borrowed words get forced through the borrower\'s sound system — phonology is the immigration officer.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/linguistics/intermediate/phonology">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Phonology studies <strong>how languages organise sounds into systems</strong> — which sound
    differences carry meaning (phonemes), which are predictable variants (allophones), and the
    rules that govern where each appears. The same physical sounds play completely different
    roles in different languages.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="phonemes, allophones, rules, and what loanwords reveal" widget="equationStep" config={phonologyCfg} />

  <h2>The classic phenomena</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Minimal pairs:</strong> pin/bin, sip/zip — the testing ground for identifying phonemes. One sound difference, one meaning difference.</li>
    <li><strong>Assimilation and deletion:</strong> sounds influence neighbours — "input" often said as "imput" (n before p assimilates); fast speech deletes syllables ("camera" → "camra"). Rules run automatically, below awareness.</li>
    <li><strong>The wug test (Berko, 1958):</strong> children pluralise nonsense "wug" → "wugs" correctly — proving they know the RULE, not just memorised words. The founding demonstration of children's internal grammar.</li>
    <li><strong>Phonotactics:</strong> every language restricts sound sequences — "blick" is a possible English word, "bnick" is not. Speakers know the rules without ever learning them explicitly.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Berko, Word (1958); Hayes, Introductory Phonology; Odden, Introducing Phonology.</p>
</TopicPage>
